-- Q3891: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The general public, potential investors, and any interested party","Only the service organization''s board of directors and executives","Management of the service organization, user entities, and prospective user entities","Only the service auditor''s firm and its affiliated offices"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AICPA SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), SOC 2 reports are restricted-use reports intended for management of the service organization, user entities, and prospective user entities with sufficient knowledge to interpret the report. This restriction ensures readers have adequate context for the detailed technical content.
Wrong (A): The general public is not an intended audience for SOC 2 reports; SOC 3 reports are the general-use alternative designed for public distribution.
Wrong (B): The audience is broader than just the board; user entities and prospective user entities are also intended recipients.
Wrong (D): The report is not limited to the service auditor''s firm; its primary purpose is to serve user entities and their auditors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3891;

-- Q3895: SOC Reporting [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Using different methods is not permitted; one method must apply uniformly","The service auditor must issue two separate reports for each method","The report must default to carve-out for both subservice organizations","The report includes the network security provider''s controls in scope while carving out the data center operator"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AT-C Section 320.14, a service organization may use different subservice organization presentation methods within the same SOC report. The inclusive method brings the network security provider''s controls into scope for testing, while the carve-out method excludes the data center operator and identifies expected CSOCs.
Wrong (A): AT-C 320 permits mixing methods within a single report based on engagement circumstances.
Wrong (B): A single SOC report can accommodate both methods; separate reports are not required.
Wrong (C): The inclusive method used for one subservice organization does not force carve-out for the other.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3895;

-- Q3896: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Marketing materials, customer testimonials, and competitive positioning statements","Engagement scope, management''s responsibilities, auditor''s responsibilities, criteria, and opinion","User entities'' financial statements and the auditor''s opinion on fair presentation","Employee handbook, organizational chart, and compensation structure details"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C Section 320.53, the independent service auditor''s report must include the engagement scope, management''s responsibilities for the system description and controls, the auditor''s responsibilities, the criteria used, and the auditor''s opinion. These elements provide the structured foundation for user entities'' reliance on the report.
Wrong (A): Marketing materials and testimonials are not part of the auditor''s report and fall outside the engagement scope.
Wrong (C): SOC 2 reports do not address user entities'' financial statements; that is the domain of financial statement audits.
Wrong (D): Internal HR documents like handbooks and compensation structures are not relevant to the service auditor''s report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3896;

-- Q3897: SOC Reporting [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Exceptions are isolated, immaterial to trust services criteria, and do not indicate systemic failures","Any exception automatically requires a qualified opinion regardless of materiality","Exceptions are removed from the report so it presents only effective controls","The report must be reissued after remediation and retesting of corrected controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AT-C 205, an unqualified opinion can be issued even when minor exceptions exist, provided the exceptions are not material to the trust services criteria and do not represent systemic control failures. The auditor evaluates whether exceptions are isolated incidents or indicate a pattern of weakness.
Wrong (B): Materiality and systemic nature are the determining factors, not the mere existence of exceptions; isolated deviations do not automatically require qualification.
Wrong (C): Exceptions are disclosed in the report, not removed; transparency about findings is a core requirement of SOC reporting.
Wrong (D): The auditor opines on the period examined; reissuance after remediation is not a standard requirement for the current report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3897;

-- Q3900: SOC Reporting [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The service auditor''s fee structure and billing arrangements for the engagement","The total page count and formatting standards used in the report","The service organization''s recent stock price performance and market position","The service auditor''s competence, independence, report scope, period, and noted exceptions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C Section 402, before relying on a SOC 1 Type II report, the user entity''s auditor must evaluate the service auditor''s professional competence and independence, whether the report covers the relevant period and scope, and any reported exceptions. The auditor must also assess complementary user entity controls (CUECs).
Wrong (A): The service auditor''s fees are irrelevant to the user auditor''s evaluation of report reliability and sufficiency.
Wrong (B): Page count and formatting have no bearing on the report''s usefulness or the auditor''s ability to rely on it.
Wrong (C): Stock price performance is unrelated to the evaluation of controls at the service organization.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3900;

-- Q3904: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Services unrelated to the engagement scope that do not affect evaluated criteria","Access control procedures for the in-scope application and its components","Infrastructure components that support the in-scope services being reported","People and procedures directly involved in delivering the in-scope services"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AICPA SOC Guidance (DC Section 200) and AT-C Section 320.35, the system description must include all components relevant to in-scope services, while services outside the engagement scope that do not affect the criteria may be appropriately excluded.
Wrong (B): Access control procedures for in-scope applications are directly relevant to the criteria and must be included in the system description.
Wrong (C): Infrastructure supporting in-scope services is part of the system boundary and must be described in the report.
Wrong (D): People and procedures involved in delivering in-scope services are required system description components under AT-C 320.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3904;

-- Q3905: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both use the same terminology as established by the PCAOB standards","There is no real difference; the terms are fully interchangeable","SOC 1 uses criteria while SOC 2 uses control objectives instead","SOC 1 objectives are defined by management for ICFR; SOC 2 criteria are AICPA Trust Services"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): In SOC 1 engagements, management defines control objectives describing what controls should achieve for user entities'' internal control over financial reporting. In SOC 2 engagements, the criteria are the predefined Trust Services Criteria (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy) established by the AICPA.
Wrong (A): The PCAOB does not establish SOC terminology; these are AICPA standards under SSAE 18.
Wrong (B): The terms reflect fundamentally different concepts appropriate to each engagement type and are not interchangeable.
Wrong (C): This reverses the terminology; SOC 1 uses control objectives and SOC 2 uses criteria, not the other way around.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3905;

-- Q3907: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether controls operated effectively over a specified period of time","Whether the description is fairly presented and controls are suitably designed","Whether the service organization''s financial statements are materially correct","Whether user entities have implemented all required complementary controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320.39 (SSAE No. 18), the Type I opinion addresses whether the system description fairly presents the system as designed and implemented as of a specified date, and whether controls are suitably designed to achieve the stated criteria. Type I is a point-in-time assessment of design suitability only.
Wrong (A): Operating effectiveness over a period is addressed by a Type II report, not a Type I report.
Wrong (C): SOC 2 reports do not address the service organization''s financial statements; that falls under financial statement audits.
Wrong (D): Complementary user entity controls are the user entity''s responsibility and are not covered by the service auditor''s opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3907;

-- Q3908: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the exceptions'' significance, assess risk impact, and request remediation","Accept the qualified report without taking any further action on it","Immediately terminate the contract and migrate to a different provider","Ignore the change since the user entity bears no responsibility for provider controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AICPA SOC 2 guidance and AT-C Section 320, the user entity should evaluate the specific exceptions underlying the qualified opinion, assess their potential impact on operations and risk profile, and request a remediation plan with timelines from the service organization.
Wrong (B): Accepting a qualified report without evaluation could leave the user entity exposed to unmitigated risks from the identified control deficiencies.
Wrong (C): Immediate termination is a disproportionate response; a measured assessment of the exceptions'' significance should precede any contract decisions.
Wrong (D): User entities retain responsibility for monitoring their service providers'' control environments and cannot ignore changes in opinion status.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3908;

-- Q3911: IT General Controls [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A physical lock restricting access to the data center facility door","Annual security awareness training for all IT department employees","A written policy statement posted prominently on the company intranet","Technical enforcement preventing direct production changes without documented approval"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per COBIT 2019 BAI06 (Manage Changes) and AICPA TSC CC8.1, technical enforcement controls that prevent production changes without a corresponding approved change ticket provide stronger assurance than administrative policies alone. Automated enforcement blocks unauthorized changes at the system level regardless of urgency or role.
Wrong (A): Physical locks secure facility access but do not prevent a DBA with legitimate system access from bypassing change management processes.
Wrong (B): Training is an administrative control that raises awareness but cannot technically prevent an individual from making unauthorized changes.
Wrong (C): A posted policy communicates expectations but lacks technical enforcement; individuals can still bypass it under pressure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3911;

-- Q3912: IT General Controls [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It fully replaces the need for physical inventory counts of assets","It provides a centralized record of assets, configurations, and relationships for ITGC","It automatically detects and fixes configuration errors in production systems","It generates licensing revenue by selling asset data to external third parties"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT BAI10 (Manage Configuration) and ITIL Service Asset and Configuration Management, a CMDB provides a comprehensive inventory of IT assets, configurations, dependencies, and relationships. This centralized repository supports change impact analysis, incident root cause identification, and access control scoping.
Wrong (A): A CMDB supplements but does not replace physical inventory counts; periodic reconciliation with physical assets is still necessary.
Wrong (C): A CMDB records configuration data but does not automatically remediate errors; it is a reference tool, not an active control.
Wrong (D): CMDBs are internal governance tools; generating revenue from asset data is not their purpose.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3912;

-- Q3913: IT Audit Frameworks [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No documentation is required for risk acceptance decisions by management","Only an informal email from the IT manager mentioning the identified risk","A formal risk acceptance record signed by management with rationale and review date","A vendor proposal outlining the cost of the replacement system upgrade"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-39 Section 3.2 and COBIT APO12.04, risk acceptance decisions must be formally documented with a risk acceptance statement identifying the specific risk, rationale for acceptance, residual risk level, authorizing manager, and a reassessment date. This demonstrates due diligence and supports audit accountability.
Wrong (A): Governance frameworks require formal documentation for all risk treatment decisions, including acceptance.
Wrong (B): An informal email lacks the rigor, traceability, and management sign-off required by risk management frameworks.
Wrong (D): A vendor proposal addresses potential remediation cost but does not document the organization''s formal acceptance of residual risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3913;

-- Q3914: SOC Testing Controls [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The missed reconciliation is immaterial since 11 of 12 months were completed","The auditor should evaluate the deviation''s cause, impact, and compensating procedures","The auditor should automatically issue a qualified opinion for the exception","The auditor should disregard the gap since the control operated most of the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320.35), a missed occurrence of a periodic control represents a deviation that the service auditor must evaluate for cause, impact, and materiality. The auditor assesses whether the missed reconciliation was isolated or systemic, and whether compensating procedures mitigated the gap.
Wrong (A): One deviation out of twelve cannot be automatically dismissed as immaterial; the auditor must evaluate its cause and significance.
Wrong (C): A single missed occurrence does not automatically require a qualified opinion; materiality and systemic nature determine the impact on the opinion.
Wrong (D): The auditor cannot disregard any control deviation; AT-C 320 requires evaluation of all exceptions regardless of frequency.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3914;

-- Q3915: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Test every transaction processed by the control during the examination period","No testing is required for any automated controls in a SOC engagement","Test a random sample of 60 items drawn from throughout the examination period","Test at one point in time and confirm the code was unchanged during the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320.A40 (SSAE No. 18), automated controls embedded in application code operate consistently as long as the code remains unchanged. The auditor can test at a single point in time and verify through change management records that no modifications were made during the period.
Wrong (A): Testing every transaction is unnecessarily extensive for a consistently operating automated control with effective ITGCs.
Wrong (B): Automated controls still require testing; the auditor must verify the control functions as intended and that the code was not modified.
Wrong (C): Sample-based testing applies to manual controls with variability; automated controls warrant point-in-time testing with change verification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3915;

-- Q3919: SOC Reporting [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Express an opinion covering the other information section of the report","Read it for material inconsistencies but not express an opinion on it","Remove the other information section before the report is formally issued","Ignore the other information section entirely without any review process"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AT-C Section 320, the service auditor reads the other information section to identify material inconsistencies with the audited system description or apparent material misstatements of fact, but does not express an opinion on it. If inconsistencies are found, the auditor discusses them with management.
Wrong (A): The auditor''s opinion does not extend to the other information section; it covers only the system description and controls.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to remove content from the report; the other information section is management''s responsibility.
Wrong (D): The auditor has a professional responsibility to read the section for inconsistencies even though no opinion is issued on it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3919;

-- Q4919: Change Management [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The control is effective because 92.5% compliance exceeds the accepted threshold","The deviation should be reported and evaluated as a potential control deficiency","The finding is immaterial because only three individual changes were affected","The entire change management process should be deemed completely ineffective"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, identified control deviations must be reported and evaluated to determine whether they represent a deficiency, significant deficiency, or material weakness. The auditor cannot simply apply a percentage threshold to dismiss deviations without evaluating their nature and impact.
Wrong (A): A percentage compliance rate does not automatically validate control effectiveness; the nature of the deviations matters more than their frequency.
Wrong (C): Materiality is not determined solely by the number of affected changes; the auditor must assess the risk significance of each deviation.
Wrong (D): Isolated deviations do not necessarily render the entire process ineffective; the evaluation should be proportionate to the findings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4919;

-- Q4921: Change Management [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inability to identify recurring problems and improve the change process over time","Increased hardware procurement costs due to untracked system components","Failure to submit change requests by the required deadline each month","Loss of CMDB backup data from the configuration management system"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT BAI06 and ITIL change management practices, without post-implementation reviews, the organization cannot identify recurring problems, measure change effectiveness, or implement continuous improvement. This creates a systemic gap in the change management lifecycle.
Wrong (B): Hardware procurement costs are a budgeting concern unrelated to the primary risk of omitting post-implementation reviews.
Wrong (C): Timely submission of change requests is a process compliance issue, not a consequence of missing post-implementation reviews.
Wrong (D): CMDB backup data loss is an infrastructure risk unrelated to the review phase of the change management process.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4921;

-- Q4922: Change Management [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Change management is an ITGC category ensuring system modifications are authorized and tested","Change management completely replaces the need for all other IT general controls","IT general controls are a subset of the change management procedures framework","Change management applies only to application-level changes, not infrastructure changes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT BAI06 and AU-C 315, change management is one category of IT general controls that ensures modifications to systems and applications are properly authorized, tested, and documented before implementation. It operates alongside other ITGC categories such as access controls and operations controls.
Wrong (B): Change management is one component of ITGCs, not a replacement; access controls, operations, and other categories remain independently necessary.
Wrong (C): The relationship is reversed; change management is a subset of ITGCs, not the other way around.
Wrong (D): Change management applies to infrastructure, operating systems, databases, and networks in addition to application-level changes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4922;

-- Q4923: Change Management [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It completely eliminates the need for rollback procedures during deployment","It allows halting the rollout and fixing issues before they affect remaining units","It provides a guarantee that no data will be lost during the migration process","It significantly reduces the total cost of the deployment across all units"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT BAI06 (Manage Changes) and ITIL release management practices, a phased rollout limits the blast radius of errors by deploying to one unit at a time. When issues arise, the organization can halt deployment, resolve the problem, and prevent the error from propagating to remaining business units.
Wrong (A): Phased rollouts still require rollback procedures; they limit exposure but do not eliminate the need for recovery planning.
Wrong (C): Phased deployment reduces data loss risk but cannot guarantee zero data loss; separate data validation controls are needed.
Wrong (D): Phased rollouts may actually increase total deployment cost due to extended timelines; the primary benefit is risk containment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4923;

-- Q4935: Data Protection Technologies [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To calculate the total cost of data storage infrastructure for the platform","To benchmark the platform''s performance metrics against industry competitors","To identify, assess, and mitigate privacy risks to individuals before processing","To determine the number of database administrators needed for operations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GDPR Article 35, a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) is required for high-risk processing and systematically identifies, assesses, and mitigates privacy risks to data subjects before processing begins. It evaluates necessity, proportionality, and compliance measures for the proposed processing activity.
Wrong (A): Infrastructure cost calculations are a budgeting exercise unrelated to the privacy risk assessment objectives of a DPIA.
Wrong (B): Competitive benchmarking addresses market positioning, not the regulatory privacy risk analysis that DPIAs are designed to perform.
Wrong (D): Staffing decisions are an operational planning matter and are not a purpose of a DPIA under data protection regulations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4935;

-- Q4939: Data Protection Technologies [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["AES-256 is insufficient encryption strength for protecting backup data at rest","Off-site storage facilities lack the capability to handle encrypted tape media","Backup tapes are not technically compatible with AES-256 encryption algorithms","Stolen tapes would provide both the encrypted data and the decryption keys together"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-111 (Guide to Storage Encryption Technologies), encryption keys must be stored separately from the data they protect. Storing keys on the same tapes as the encrypted data defeats the purpose of encryption because an attacker who obtains the tapes has everything needed to decrypt the data.
Wrong (A): AES-256 is widely recognized as a strong encryption standard suitable for backup data; the algorithm strength is not the issue here.
Wrong (B): Off-site storage facilities routinely handle encrypted media; there is no technical incompatibility with encrypted tapes.
Wrong (C): Backup tapes fully support AES-256 encryption; modern tape drives include hardware encryption capabilities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4939;

-- Q4948: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["SOC 1 addresses ICFR controls; SOC 2 addresses trust services criteria","SOC 1 reports are always Type I while SOC 2 reports are always Type II","SOC 1 reports are public documents while SOC 2 reports are restricted-use","SOC 2 reports apply only to cloud service providers, not other organizations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): SOC 1 reports (under SSAE 18 / AT-C 320) evaluate controls relevant to user entities'' internal control over financial reporting (ICFR). SOC 2 reports (under AT-C 205) evaluate controls based on the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy).
Wrong (B): Both SOC 1 and SOC 2 can be issued as either Type I (point-in-time) or Type II (period-of-time) reports.
Wrong (C): Both SOC 1 and SOC 2 reports are restricted-use reports; SOC 3 is the general-use public report.
Wrong (D): SOC 2 reports apply to any service organization, not exclusively cloud providers; they cover any entity providing services to user entities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4948;

-- Q4950: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A requirement to corroborate every management assertion with external evidence","An assumption that management is dishonest until independently proven otherwise","A refusal to accept any representations or claims provided by management","A questioning mind, alertness to possible misstatement, and critical evidence assessment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 200.14, professional skepticism is an attitude that includes a questioning mind, alertness to conditions that may indicate possible misstatement due to fraud or error, and a critical assessment of audit evidence. It requires an objective, questioning approach throughout the engagement.
Wrong (A): Skepticism does not require external corroboration of every assertion; the auditor exercises judgment about the sufficiency of evidence.
Wrong (B): Professional skepticism presumes neither honesty nor dishonesty; it maintains a neutral, questioning stance rather than assuming guilt.
Wrong (C): The auditor can accept management representations as one form of evidence; skepticism means evaluating them critically, not rejecting them entirely.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4950;

-- Q4951: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The subservice organization''s controls are included in the SOC report scope and tested","The subservice organization is no longer responsible for any of its controls","The subservice organization''s controls are excluded from scope with functions identified","The service auditor directly tests the subservice organization''s controls for effectiveness"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 320, the carve-out method excludes the subservice organization''s controls from the service organization''s SOC report scope. The report identifies the nature of services provided and functions performed by the subservice organization, but does not include testing of its controls. User entities would need a separate SOC report from the subservice organization.
Wrong (A): This describes the inclusive method, not the carve-out method; under carve-out, the subservice organization''s controls are excluded.
Wrong (B): The subservice organization retains full responsibility for its controls; the carve-out merely excludes them from this particular report''s scope.
Wrong (D): Under carve-out, the service auditor does not test the subservice organization''s controls; that would occur under the inclusive method.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4951;

-- Q4954: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["SOC 3 reports are restricted to user entities while SOC 2 reports are publicly available","Both SOC 2 and SOC 3 report types have identical distribution restrictions","SOC 2 reports are restricted-use while SOC 3 reports are general-use and publicly available","SOC 3 reports are available only to regulatory agencies and government entities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AT-C Section 320 and SOC 3 guidance, SOC 2 reports are restricted-use reports intended for user entities, their auditors, and prospective user entities. SOC 3 reports are general-use reports that can be freely distributed, including posting on a company''s website for marketing purposes.
Wrong (A): This reverses the distribution rules; SOC 2 reports are restricted and SOC 3 reports are publicly available, not the opposite.
Wrong (B): The two report types have fundamentally different distribution models; SOC 2 is restricted-use while SOC 3 is general-use.
Wrong (D): SOC 3 reports are available to the general public, not limited to regulatory agencies; their purpose is broader public assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4954;

-- Q4955: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The bridge period, which may require a bridge letter from the service organization","The testing window used to define the service auditor''s sampling approach","The remediation period for addressing control deficiencies found in the report","The pre-engagement phase covering planning activities before fieldwork begins"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C Section 402, the gap between the end of a SOC report period and the user entity''s fiscal year-end is known as the bridge period (or gap period). The user entity auditor may need to obtain a bridge letter from the service organization or perform additional procedures to address the uncovered period.
Wrong (B): The testing window refers to the examination period covered by the SOC report itself, not the gap after the report period ends.
Wrong (C): The remediation period is not a standard term; control deficiencies are addressed through management''s corrective action plans, not a defined period.
Wrong (D): The pre-engagement phase occurs before the SOC engagement begins and is unrelated to the gap between report period and fiscal year-end.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4955;

-- Q4956: SOC Report Content and Structure [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The description contains no information whatsoever about any system weaknesses","The description includes every possible technical detail about the infrastructure","The description has been reviewed and formally approved by external legal counsel","The description meets criteria and does not omit or distort relevant information"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320, "fairly presents" means the system description is presented in accordance with the description criteria and does not omit or distort information relevant to the scope of the engagement. It conveys an accurate and complete picture of the system within the defined engagement boundaries.
Wrong (A): A fair presentation does not require omitting weaknesses; relevant weaknesses within scope should be disclosed, not hidden.
Wrong (B): Fairly presents does not mean exhaustive technical detail; it means the description is accurate, complete, and relevant to the engagement scope.
Wrong (C): External legal counsel approval is not a requirement of fair presentation; the standard is adherence to the description criteria established by the AICPA.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4956;

-- Q4957: SOC Report Content and Structure [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether controls were operating effectively throughout a specified examination period","Whether all user entities have implemented their required complementary controls","Whether the service organization fully complies with applicable GDPR requirements","Whether the description is fairly presented and controls are suitably designed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320.39, the SOC 2 Type I opinion addresses whether the system description fairly presents the system as designed and implemented as of a specified date, and whether controls are suitably designed to meet the applicable trust services criteria. It is a point-in-time design assessment.
Wrong (A): Operating effectiveness over a period is addressed by Type II reports, not Type I; Type I evaluates design suitability only.
Wrong (B): Complementary user entity controls are the user entity''s responsibility and fall outside the scope of the service auditor''s opinion.
Wrong (C): GDPR compliance is not directly within the scope of a SOC 2 Type I opinion; the report evaluates controls against trust services criteria.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4957;

-- Q4959: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Controls the user entity is expected to implement at its own facilities","Controls required by trust services criteria but not implemented by any party","Controls that the service auditor implements during the testing procedures","Controls the service organization assumes the subservice organization has in place"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AT-C Section 320, complementary subservice organization controls (CSOCs) are controls that the service organization assumes the subservice organization has implemented for the overall control objectives or criteria to be achieved. These are identified in the system description when the carve-out method is used.
Wrong (A): Controls the user entity is expected to implement are called complementary user entity controls (CUECs), not CSOCs.
Wrong (B): Controls required but not implemented by any party represent a control gap, not a defined category of complementary controls.
Wrong (C): The service auditor tests controls but does not implement them; the auditor''s role is to evaluate, not operate controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4959;

-- Q4960: SOC Report Content and Structure [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The report should have been split into two separate reports for each environment","Data center changes are not within SOC 2 scope and can be disregarded entirely","The auditor should evaluate whether controls were tested before and after migration","The mid-period migration automatically invalidates the service auditor''s opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AT-C Section 320, when a material system change such as a data center migration occurs during the examination period, the user entity auditor should evaluate whether the service auditor tested controls in both the pre- and post-migration environments. This ensures control effectiveness was assessed across the entire period despite the environmental change.
Wrong (A): A single SOC 2 report can cover a period that includes material system changes; splitting into two reports is not required.
Wrong (B): Data center infrastructure is within SOC 2 scope when it supports the system described under the trust services criteria; it cannot be disregarded.
Wrong (D): A mid-period migration does not automatically invalidate the opinion; it requires the auditor to address both environments in testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4960;
